## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP with non-overlapping selections and per-subarray costs, mirroring real scenarios like batch processing, budget allocation, or ad-slot optimization where exactly k intervals are chosen from a sequence.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping k-interval DP
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Maintaining a running best to optimize transitions from O(n) to O(1)
- Reconstruction of exact intervals

## DP Recurrence
Let pref[i] = sum of first i elements (pref[0] = 0).

**Transition:**
dp[i][t] = max(
  dp[i-1][t],                                    // Don't end a subarray at i
  best[t] + pref[i]                              // End a subarray at i
)

where best[t] = max_{1 ≤ l ≤ i} (dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1])

**Initialization:**
dp[0][0] = 0, dp[0][t>0] = -∞

**Answer:** dp[n][k]

## Reconstruction
Track which choice was made at each dp[i][t]. If dp[i][t] came from dp[i-1][t], no subarray ends at i. Otherwise, find l where dp[l-1][t-1] - pref[l-1] = best[t], and record subarray (l..i).

## Complexity
- Time: O(nk) with O(1) transitions using running best
- Space: O(nk) for dp table, O(nk) for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = [0];
  for (let x of A) pref.push(pref[pref.length - 1] + x);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip position i
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 0; // 0 = skip
      }

      // Start/extend subarray ending at i
      if (t > 0) {
        let best = -Infinity;
        for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]) - c;
          if (val > best) best = val;
        }
        if (best > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = best;
          choice[i][t] = 1; // 1 = take subarray ending at i
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const subarrays = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (choice[i][t] === 1) {
      let l = 1;
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[j - 1]) - c;
        if (val === dp[i][t]) {
          l = j;
          break;
        }
      }
      subarrays.unshift([l - 1, i - 1]); // Convert to 0-indexed
      t--;
    }
    i--;
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays };
}
```

## Optimized O(nk) Version
The inner loop can be optimized by maintaining a running best:
```javascript
for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    best[t] = Math.max(best[t], dp[i - 1][t - 1] - pref[i - 1]);
    dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], best[t] + pref[i] - c);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests advanced tree DP with edge deletions and a non-trivial objective—maximizing the sum of component maxima. It requires careful state design, knapsack-style merging, and the ability to reconstruct the solution from computed values.

## Key Concepts
- **Tree DP with edge cuts**: Model each edge cut as separating a subtree into a new component
- **Knapsack-like merging over children**: Combine subproblems using convolution-style DP
- **Maintaining component maxima**: Track both the sum (dp) and the current component's max weight (mx)

## Code Example
```javascript
function solve(n, edges, weights, k) {
    const children = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
    for (const [u, v] of edges) children[u - 1].push(v - 1);

    const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const mx = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9));
    const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);

    function dfs(u) {
        dp[u][0] = 0;
        mx[u][0] = weights[u];
        for (const v of children[u]) {
            parent[v] = u;
            dfs(v);
            for (let t = k; t >= 0; t--) {
                const ndp = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                const nmx = new Array(k + 1).fill(-1e9);
                for (let t1 = 0; t1 <= t; t1++) {
                    const t2 = t - t1;
                    // cut edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2] + mx[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], mx[u][t1]);
                    // keep edge
                    ndp[t] = Math.max(ndp[t], dp[u][t1] + dp[v][t2]);
                    nmx[t] = Math.max(nmx[t], Math.max(mx[u][t1], mx[v][t2]));
                }
                dp[u] = ndp;
                mx[u] = nmx;
            }
        }
    }

    dfs(0);
    return { maxSum: dp[0][k], edgesToCut: [] }; // reconstruct edges by backtracking decisions
}
```

## Common Pitfalls
- **Forgetting to reset mx when cutting**: When an edge is cut, the parent component's max weight remains unchanged
- **Incorrect knapsack direction**: Always iterate cuts in descending order to avoid reusing the same child multiple times
- **Off-by-one in component counting**: k edge cuts always produce k+1 components

